.TH ARMSTAT 8 "October 8, 2026" "armstat" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
armstat \- ARM64 server interval monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B armstat
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B armstat
samples CPU frequency, Busy/Idle, idle states, power, temperature, PMU counters,
and IPC through Linux procfs, sysfs, hwmon, and perf interfaces.
The initial target is Huawei Kunpeng; each deployment model needs physical
hardware acceptance testing.
.PP
Default output contains one \fBSUM\fR row.
\fB-a\fR adds CPU rows and live metrics, including CPU topology.
\fB-s all\fR selects the full base view with package rows, configuration,
and idle-state usage rates.
\fB-S\fR emits only the summary. Explicit \fB-s\fR selection determines
which scopes are emitted. PMU and IPC require an explicit request.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-i ", " \-\-interval " seconds"
Requested interval, default 1.0. Accepted range: 0.000001 to 31536000 seconds.
Formulas use the measured duration.
.TP
.BI \-n ", " \-\-num-iterations " count"
Number of visible samples. Zero, the default, runs until interrupted.
.TP
.BI \-N ", " \-\-header-iterations " count"
Repeat text headers every N samples. Zero prints them at startup and on
layout changes.
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-cpu " list"
Sample real Linux CPU IDs, for example \fB0,1,4-7\fR. Invalid lists or lists
matching no online CPU are startup errors.
.TP
.BI \-B ", " \-\-busy-source " source"
Select \fBauto\fR, \fBprocstat\fR, \fBschedstat\fR, or \fBtask-clock\fR.
Default: auto. See METRICS.
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-output " file"
Write output to a file.
.TP
.BI \-O ", " \-\-export " file"
Alias of \fB-o\fR for text, JSON, or CSV output.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Omit the text banner, headers, timestamps, and interval markers.
.TP
.B \-D, \-\-dump
Measure one complete interval and exit. Text retains headers unless
\fB-q\fR is also specified.
.TP
.B \-S, \-\-summary
Emit one SUM row or object per interval.
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-all
Expand live metrics with CPU rows and Pkg/Core/Node topology. Omit package
aggregate rows, Min/Max/Governor/Boost, and LPI usage rates; use \fB-s all\fR
for the full base view. Neither view enables PMU or IPC.
.TP
.BI \-f ", " \-\-format " format"
Output format: \fBtext\fR (default), \fBjson\fR, or \fBcsv\fR.
.TP
.BI \-s ", " \-\-show " items"
Select comma-separated groups or exact fields. The first option starts a
whitelist, retaining explicit \fB-p\fR, \fB-I\fR, and \fB-J\fR requests.
Repeated options form a union, including the full \fB-s all\fR selection,
without restoring unrelated groups hidden by \fB-H\fR.
.TP
.BI \-H ", " \-\-hide " items"
Hide comma-separated groups or exact fields.
.TP
.BI \-p ", " \-\-pmu-events " events"
Select comma-separated PMU events. Unknown or duplicate names and lists over
16 events are startup errors. Unavailable known events remain visible.
.TP
.B \-I, \-\-ipc
Select cycles and instructions and enable IPC.
.TP
.B \-J, \-\-joules
Show interval energy in joules.
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-list
List groups, field IDs, scopes, types, units, labels, JSON keys, and PMU names.
Stable field IDs are recommended for scripts.
.TP
.B \-P, \-\-probe
Report topology, data sources, candidate counts, Busy/Idle policy, idle-state
names, and basic PMU availability. The key-value contract has
\fBprobe_schema_version: 1\fR. The PMU check opens cycles on the first selected
CPU only; it does not verify every event on every CPU.
Discovery is independent of \fB-s\fR/\fB-H\fR selection.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-debug
Show initialization and runtime diagnostics.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print command-line help and exit.
.SH COLUMN SELECTION
Groups are \fBcpu\fR, \fBpkg/package\fR, \fBcore\fR, \fBnuma/node\fR,
\fBfreq\fR, \fBfreq-config\fR, \fBidle\fR, \fBidle-usage\fR,
\fBpower\fR, \fBtemp\fR, \fBpmu\fR,
\fBsysstat/irq\fR, \fBmembw/mem\fR, \fBipc\fR, \fBenergy/joules\fR,
and \fBall\fR. A slash separates aliases, not literal option syntax.
Group names are case-insensitive and take precedence. Other stable field IDs
match one field before text/JSON aliases; shared aliases do not expand row
scopes. CPU-only aliases such as \fBMin\fR and \fBlpi0_usage\fR enable CPU rows.
Use \fB--list\fR for the complete registry and stable IDs such as
\fBfreq_mhz\fR, \fBsummary_freq_mhz\fR, and \fBpkg_freq_mhz\fR.
.PP
\fBfreq\fR selects current frequency and UncoreFreq; \fBfreq-config\fR selects
Min/Max/Governor/Boost. \fBidle\fR selects LPI residency and Busy/Idle/IOWait;
\fBidle-usage\fR selects state-entry rates. Core, numa, freq-config, and
idle-usage automatically enable CPU rows.
\fB-s cpu\fR alone supplies common per-CPU frequency, utilization, LPI residency,
and temperature without SUM. Combined selections use the requested fields and
scopes. These defaults preserve explicit \fB-H\fR exclusions.
Independent \fB-p\fR, \fB-I\fR, and \fB-J\fR requests retain required SUM
output, subject to CPU-filter rules.
The first \fB-s\fR replaces earlier view settings: \fB-a -s cpu\fR selects
the CPU shortcut; \fB-s cpu -a\fR selects the expanded live view.
.PP
Package rows are hidden by default; select \fBpkg\fR, \fBpackage\fR,
or an exact field such as \fBpkg_freq_mhz\fR to request them. CPU package
topology remains available for identification and plotting.
Unknown items and selections that cannot emit any row fail at startup.
If capability discovery leaves the selected view with only CPU row IDs and
no data columns, startup fails.
.PP
\fB--cpu\fR filters collection and CPU-derived aggregates, including perf
resources. Global counters and platform sensors retain their own scope.
The default summary uses the filtered CPUs. Explicitly expanding CPU rows
with a CPU filter suppresses implicit aggregate sections; use
\fB-S --cpu\fR for a filtered summary. Explicit aggregate-only selections
remain available.
.SH OUTPUT
The first result follows one complete interval. Sampling uses monotonic
clock deadlines; an overrun skips missed deadlines instead of issuing catch-up
bursts. CPU hotplug rebuilds dependent state and establishes a new baseline
before output resumes.
.PP
When only SUM is emitted, text uses one timestamped data row per interval.
Other views retain interval blocks with sample number, timezone-qualified
sample-end timestamp, and measured duration. Text timestamps keep the date
and timezone at millisecond precision. Headers include units; \fB/int\fR means
a count per interval.
Headers repeat when columns or widths change, including sparse package IDs.
.PP
CPU rows use real Linux IDs and are sorted by ID. Package rows group selected
CPUs by physical package ID. SUM averages valid CPU frequency and Busy/Idle
values. Each SUM LPI state requires valid data from every selected CPU; summary
PMU likewise requires every CPU group and sums their interval counts.
.SH METRICS
.TP
.B Frequency
\fBFreq\fR is a cpuinfo_cur_freq point sample in MHz. Summary and package
frequency are averages of current CPU samples, not interval-average or busy
frequencies. \fBMin\fR and \fBMax\fR are configured scaling limits.
\fBGovernor\fR and \fBBoost\fR report cached configuration. Summary
\fBUncoreFreq\fR is a unique matching devfreq cur_freq sample in MHz.
.TP
.B Busy/Idle
Auto and procstat use \fB/proc/stat\fR on all CPUs, including nohz_full.
Idle% is the idle-plus-iowait delta divided by measured duration; Busy% is
100 minus Idle%. IOWait% separately reports the iowait delta and is already
excluded from procstat Busy%. Short intervals may show USER_HZ quantization.
Schedstat uses per-CPU runtime with procstat fallback and is diagnostic only:
runtime settled at task switches can omit ongoing execution. Task-clock is
its compatibility alias.
.TP
.B Idle states
\fBLPI-N\fR is cpuidle stateN/time delta divided by measured duration, times
100. \fBLPI-N_usage\fR is per-CPU stateN/usage delta per second.
States are independent of Idle% and column visibility; no residual is
redistributed and there is no default IdleGap column. States 0 through 7 are
exposed. \fB--probe\fR maps each field to the kernel state name.
Time settles on state exit, so a long idle period may report zero in earlier
windows and more than 100% on exit. Values are not capped. Software accounting
does not prove the hardware reached the requested state. Missing, disabled,
failed, or reset states are independently unavailable.
.TP
.B Power and energy
\fBPower\fR estimates interval mean power from endpoint package-sensor samples,
in mW, shown in SUM. \fBEnergy\fR estimates interval joules from this mean
and measured duration; it is not a cumulative energy counter.
.TP
.B Temperature
\fBTemp0\fR through \fBTemp3\fR follow the NUMA/die sensor mapping in
ENVIRONMENT. CPU \fBTemp\fR uses the CPU's NUMA temperature. Units are degrees
Celsius; this backend does not provide per-core temperature or power.
.TP
.B Other counters
\fBMemBW\fR is memory-read counter delta per second in MiB/s.
\fBCtxSw\fR, \fBIRQs\fR, \fBSoftIRQs\fR, and PMU events are interval
counts, not per-second rates. \fBIPC\fR is instructions divided by cycles,
requiring valid named events and a nonzero cycle count.
.SH PMU EVENTS
Built-in names are:
.nf
cycles, instructions, cache-references, cache-misses,
branches, branch-misses, mem-access, mem-read, mem-write,
l1d-cache-refill, l1d-cache, l1i-cache-refill, l1i-cache,
l2d-cache-refill, l2d-cache, l3d-cache-refill, l3d-cache
.fi
.PP
Raw events require 0x or 0X followed by hexadecimal digits within 64 bits;
signs and embedded whitespace are invalid. Mem-read and mem-write count
retired loads and stores, not bytes. Optional events depend on the CPU.
.PP
An explicit PMU/IPC request without an event list defaults to cycles and
instructions. \fB-p\fR alone does not add IPC. Perf events are grouped per
selected CPU and interval counts are multiplex-scaled with time_enabled and
time_running using exact integer arithmetic rounded to nearest.
Failed, short, reset, or unscheduled reads and scaling overflow make the whole
CPU group unavailable. Recovery establishes a new baseline. Summary sums
per-CPU interval deltas and rejects an overflowing sum.
.PP
PMU usually requires root or permissive perf_event_paranoid. File-descriptor
use grows with CPUs and events. Armstat attempts to raise its soft
RLIMIT_NOFILE within the hard limit; reduce CPUs or events if capacity remains
insufficient.
.SH EXPORTS
JSON and CSV use \fBschema_version=9\fR. Bundled plotting loaders accept only
that version. LPI fields contain independent sysfs statistics; version 8's
residual-adjusted values cannot be interpreted as version 9 data.
.PP
Every interval or CSV row carries schema_version, one-based interval number,
duration_us, timestamp, timestamp_ns, and timestamp_iso. The timestamps describe
the sample end; timestamp_iso is local RFC 3339 with nine fractional digits.
Use duration_us for the measured monotonic window.
.PP
JSON is an array of interval objects with selected summary, packages, and cpus
sections. Default output includes only summary; \fB-a\fR adds cpus.
\fB-s all\fR also includes packages. Machine timestamps retain nine fractional
digits and duration_us retains the measured window.
Package and CPU objects use package and cpu identity keys.
Unavailable values are null; available Boost is boolean. Strings are escaped
and non-finite numbers become null.
.PP
CSV has one header. Default and summary-only output use Scope=SUM;
package-only uses Package;
CPU-only uses CPU. Mixed output uses Scope,CPU,Package with SUM, PKG, and CPU
rows. Mixed data columns are qualified with summary., package., or cpu.;
compact columns use JSON keys. Unavailable and out-of-scope cells are empty.
Use a conforming CSV parser.
.PP
CSV layout changes stop capture before the affected interval with a nonzero
exit; restart CSV or use JSON for changing column sets. CPU membership may
change if the layout stays fixed. Existing output targets are opened only
after initialization, baseline collection, and output-buffer allocation
succeed. Later errors retain the written prefix and return nonzero.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ARMSTAT_TEMP_POLICY
Default \fBthermal-zone-index\fR maps thermal_zoneN/temp to TempN and NUMA/die
N. Sparse node IDs and signed temperatures are preserved. \fBnone\fR or
\fBdisabled\fR turns off summary temperature discovery. Unknown values fail
startup. Disable this mapping when it does not describe the server's sensors.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Power requires exactly one power_meter/power1_average source; memory bandwidth
requires exactly one mem_bytes_read source. Ambiguous discovery remains
unavailable and is reported by --probe. Without cpuidle, LPI columns are
hidden and Busy/Idle still works. Interval read failures affect dependent
fields; cumulative counters need a new baseline after recovery.
.PP
CPU IDs above 1023 are unrepresentable. Armstat warns and continues with
representable online IDs. Dedicated core aggregate rows are not implemented.
Hardware telemetry and sustained behavior require validation on the actual
server; VM execution verifies portability and contracts only.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero means the operation and checked output flush succeeded. One means invalid
arguments, initialization or collection failure, an unusable selection, or
an output write/finalization failure. Closed pipes are handled as EPIPE so
resources are cleaned up before exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
armstat --probe
armstat -i 1 -n 5
armstat -S -a -i 1 -n 10
armstat -a --cpu 0-3
armstat -s cpu
armstat -s cpu,freq,idle
armstat -s freq-config
armstat -s idle-usage
armstat -s all
armstat -s pkg_freq_mhz
armstat -I
armstat -S -a -f json -O summary.json -i 1 -n 60
armstat -a -f csv -O cpus.csv -i 1 -n 60
armstat-plot-summary summary.json --preset freq
armstat-plot-cpu cpus.csv --preset busy --top 8
.fi
.SH FILES
The installed README and integration reference are under
\fBPREFIX/share/doc/armstat\fR; the default PREFIX is /usr.
The reference covers architecture, formulas, export details, plotting, and
release acceptance. Shared plotting modules are in PREFIX/share/armstat.
